{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that centralization is more resilient because it enables coordinated, rapid responses. But it fails the problem's core distinction by ignoring correlated failure entirely in its resilience claim. Its own counterconditions concede the central control plane is an SPOF for a correlated crash and that homogeneity makes all redundant nodes vulnerable to the same 'poison pill'. These are not marginal edge-cases: they are the precise class of correlated failure the problem asks about. The target's mechanism (fast kill-switch, active-active load balancing) cannot help when the shared control plane or shared software bug is itself the trigger, so the argument asserts greater resilience while conceding the correlated-failure pathway that defeats it.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Global leap-second bug hits the large service's homogeneous kernel; the centralized control plane and active-active load balancers run the same stack, so the kill-switch and traffic-shift commands execute against nodes that fail simultaneously, leaving no healthy destination, while decentralized operators on different stacks are only partially affected."}],"target":"02355fdb5f9c1d39072a64853094e3b7f2733f9278498ca37899f2e70fa4a121"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 proposes a 'temporal-spatial signature' / 'latent synchronization' test, but its own counterconditions refute its sufficiency as evidence that would distinguish hidden-correlation from independence. When a shared cause triggers failures at widely varying intervals, the temporal signature is masked; when heterogeneity makes the shared cause produce completely different symptoms, the dependency-graph signature is obscured. These are common in real decentralized networks (independent operators have different update cadences, load, and configs), so the target's distinguishing evidence fails in exactly the high-variance regime the question targets.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A shared TLS root CA is mis-issued on day 0. Service A rotates certs immediately and fails; Service B rotates 90 days later and fails; Service C rotates 180 days later. The shared cause produces a smeared, non-clustered failure pattern that the proposed temporal-spatial signature would classify as independent, yet all three share one hidden cause."}],"target":"4774529cbe612f9daa237dd9c36b5a7701e75d0fa2f4350c2d518edaa6434cb9"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 advances the required position (decentralized > large redundant) and grounds it in a concrete mechanism: heterogeneity of execution environments across independent operators reduces fate-sharing from shared substrates (single kernel, single cloud control plane, single global config). Its counterconditions explicitly concede the cases where a shared upstream dependency or standardized deployment template reintroduces correlation. This directly addresses the problem's request to identify what distinguishes independent from merely-uncorrelated failures, so there is no genuine fault specific to this target.","target":"802299680af3779a002021acd2f9a9bc361bfe306cdc71864d703e9743b371dc"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_04 repeats SRC_002's 'temporal-spatial signature' / 'latent trigger' approach and explicitly admits its own defeating evidence: when independent operators have high variance in update cycles, the synchronization is smeared over time and 'mimics randomness'. By the target's own admission, smearing can make a hidden-cause failure look random. The proposed distinguishing evidence therefore misclassifies smeared correlated failures as independent, which is precisely the false-positive the problem asks to avoid, so the evidence is not discriminating in the heterogeneous case it claims to cover.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Same shared leap-second bug exists in all services. Operator A patches it next week and never fails; B fails immediately; C fails months later when it finally upgrades to the buggy version. The latent trigger produces no precise synchronization across the heterogeneous network, so the temporal-spatial signature would wrongly label the failures independent."}],"target":"c6e8bfa9a16a0aad97e5d0fa449a9474b5e7ab1157a0e76f1eece0cf6b5e8297"}]}